-### Parameters and Edge Cases
--   **Threshold ($T$)**: The isosurface value. Must be within the range of the scalar field.
--   **Precision**: Linear interpolation assumes the scalar field is piecewise linear within the tetrahedron. If the field is not smooth, the surface may be jagged [S1, unofficial].
--   **Degenerate Input**: If two vertices of an edge have identical scalar values, the denominator in interpolation becomes zero. A guard clause (e.g., returning the midpoint) is required [G].
--   **Boundary Conditions**: The algorithm iterates over voxels. Care must be taken at the grid boundaries to ensure all 8 vertices of a cube are within the grid bounds.
+**Parameters:**
+*   `isovalue`: The scalar threshold defining the surface. Typical values depend on the data range (e.g., 0.5 for normalized -1 to 1 data) [S2, unofficial].
+*   `winding_order`: Defines the triangle vertex ordering (e.g., `{0, 1, 2}`) [S2, unofficial].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Precision:** Linear interpolation assumes the field is linear within the tetrahedron. Non-linear fields may introduce artifacts.
+*   **Boundary Conditions:** The algorithm iterates `grid_shape - 1`, assuming the volume data includes the boundary vertices. If the volume is defined by cell centers, offsets must be adjusted [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Ambiguity Resolution:** The specific decomposition (T0-T5) is one of several possible ways to split a cube. Other decompositions exist, but this one is standard for this implementation [S2, unofficial].
