+## Subtopics
+
+### Binary MT
+A variant using binary occupancy (inside/outside) rather than scalar values, significantly faster than smoothing variants [S1, unofficial].
+
+### CA Smoothing MT
+A variant that applies smoothing to the scalar field before extraction, resulting in higher quality but much slower performance (~161.5s vs ~13-15s for Binary MT on MRI data) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
--   **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels. Each voxel is processed in constant time (6 tetrahedra, 16 cases).
--   **Memory Complexity**: $O(V + E)$, where $V$ is the number of vertices and $E$ is the number of edges in the output mesh. The memoization structure requires $O(E)$ space.
--   **Performance**: Binary MT is significantly faster than CA Smoothing MT (~13-15 seconds vs ~161.5 seconds on a 3.5M mm³ MRI dataset) [S1, unofficial].
--   **Comparison to Marching Cubes**: This method is slower and creates more vertices than standard Marching Cubes, but it better represents the mesh surface by avoiding ambiguous cases [S2, unofficial].
--   **Strengths**: Topologically correct, no disambiguation logic needed.
--   **Limitations**: Slower than Marching Cubes, more vertices.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels. Each voxel is processed once, decomposed into 6 tetrahedra.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V + E)$ where $V$ is the number of output vertices and $E$ is the number of edges. The `edge_cache` requires memory proportional to the surface area.
+*   **Performance:** Binary MT is significantly faster than CA Smoothing MT (~13-15 seconds vs ~161.5 seconds on a 3.5M mm³ MRI T1 dataset) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Comparison to Marching Cubes:** This method is slower than standard Marching Cubes and creates more vertices, but it better represents the mesh surface by avoiding topological ambiguities [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Topologically correct, watertight meshes without ambiguous cases.
+*   **Limitations:** Slower than Marching Cubes; higher vertex count.
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced as a stable surface extraction algorithm alongside Marching Cubes in the Invesalius project [S1, unofficial]. It is implemented in Rust with a Python wrapper using PyO3 [S1, unofficial]. The specific decomposition and lookup tables are derived from earlier work on Marching Tetrahedra [S2, unofficial].
+The method was introduced as a stable alternative to Marching Cubes to resolve topological ambiguities. It was implemented in the Invesalius project (Rust backend) and is also present in C# implementations like the one by Jason Fisher [S1, S2, unofficial]. The approach relies on the mathematical property that a tetrahedron cannot have the same topological ambiguity as a cube for a linear scalar field.
 
